{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision for 771e183a (G5 simulator: command coverage and the climate principal), on preparation c96ffedc and the role check 9a0f62a3.\n\n1. Command coverage: a durable scanned-through position, adopted, in this form.\n- It is demo application data under grow.sense, written by the sensors, with expected-revision fencing. No host route, no host authority, no private progress store.\n- Carry it as a field of the sensors' reading summary (the entry the simulator writes every tick anyway: \"commands answered through \u003cscanned head\u003e\"), rather than a new item and act, so the record gains no extra entries. The field holds the scanned head, not the entry that wrote it. If the summary's declared form cannot take the field, say why in the implementation note and use your separate cursor item; the rules below are the same either way.\n- A tick reads the position, scans forward from it through a cited bounded window of entries, answers every open command in journal order, then writes the position as the last scanned entry whose earlier commands are all answered. A long gap catches up over several ticks; the position never passes an unanswered command. An answer refused because the command is already closed counts as covered (the tick is idempotent after a failure); an answer refused for any other reason stops the advance, is named in the tick's own log, and the next tick retries. Every open command reserves one answer (G5a), so an unanswered older command is a leaked reservation against the scope's ceiling: coverage must be complete, and the position is the proof of it.\n- Device targetAt stays the source for the current target's outcome; the position is only for the older open commands targetAt cannot see.\n\n2. The climate principal: a distinct climate agent with its own key, adopted. This is the grow note's own member table (section 2: climate agent, controlled by the operator, vents and fans within limits, raise alerts; the note's incident closure is the agent's), not a widening. The same small Worker runs both agents, since both are controlled by the operator and section 6.3 says an agent runs wherever its controller runs it and signs with its own key; two separate secrets; the sensors key holds grow.sense and the native owner answer only, the climate key holds grow.climate only. Heat authorization stays person-only. No irrigation agent in G5.\n- Exact principal by application form, adopted as you propose: membership's single agent role lists [grow.sense, grow.climate] and the package's immutable sensors and climate party slots on Site and Room, singleton and signer-guarded, decide which agent may do which act; Site forwards the exact assignments at Room creation; the native owner is the exact sensors identity. Amend the grow note's section 3 table in a later documentation head to say the two agents share the agent role and the slots separate them (fold with the pending 6.2 amendments).\n- Runbook order: enrol both agents, then install, since G5a's install needs an active owner member. State that dependency in the runbook; the G5 request's prose order was wrong on this point.\n\n3. The story between visits. The incident count must climb when nobody is in the room, and the operator's authorize is person-only, so the declared scenario needs a path by which the fault clears without a person inside the declared period (for example the fan's trip resets after it cools and the cross-draft brings zone 2 back in band, and the agent then closes the incident and records that heater 2 was never cut). A person's authorize during the fault shortens it and is recorded as the note describes. Declare that path and its timing in the package with the cadence and period.\n\nProceed with the dependent simulator parts on this basis; the pure simulation, page and package preparation continue.","body":{"to":"builder"}}